title A fourth surface the law never named
prompt The standing fleet baton -- open the round, read the card whole, take the next agent-doable lap Lindy-first crux-first, reds first.
rota lap 4147, row 2 -- Fire, the row that sees. Its discipline is to look straight at the thing most laps would route around, then cut once at exactly the depth the truth requires. Reading it turned a carried card line into a measurement, and then the measurement into a refusal to sweep.
think THE COLD OPEN CAME BACK THREE RED AND I READ EVERY VERDICT BEFORE TOUCHING ANYTHING. 162 guards, 1,254s, tree_moved=no, guards_green=156. index_row_bound, stash_record, and standing_equipment reading its own roster's red. All three were mine and all three closed on metal, so the constructive lap came after them rather than beside them.
think THE FIRST WAS THE SHELF I HAD ALREADY LOOKED AT AND NOT SEEN. index_row_bound read rows_duplicate=2 rows_misordered=1 on session-logs/date/README-index-20260907.md. Two rows stood twice and one newer row sat below two older ones -- the %381 shape, a rebase re-applying rows without lifting the stale ones. I had read the top of that same file at the open, looking for the running thread, and read straight past the duplicate. Lifted by stamp and sorted newest-first; verdict=ok.
think THE SECOND HELD A LIVE FAULT, WHICH IS WHY AN UNLANDED RECORD IS WORTH A GATE. stash_record read unlanded=2. Both were Petrichor logs -- 20260907.050637 in stash@{0} and 20260907.023056 parked on refs/heads/pier/diverged-20260907-055442 -- and the first carried a repair that never landed. tools/fixtures/f/foundations_link_scan.sh writes /tmp/fls_links.txt and /tmp/fls_bad.txt, names fixed at write time, so every ship on this pier owns the same two paths at once and a peer's run can make a clean room read broken or a broken room read whole. HEAD still carried the constant path.
think I TOOK ONLY WHAT WAS MISSING, WHICH IS THE PART A RECOVERY GETS WRONG. Eight of the fourteen stashed paths went back: the scan's run-time pen, its control, the witness legs, the shared_pen ceiling fall, plant_witness's floor 4 -> 5, two archive shelves and the log. Two were byte-identical to HEAD and four -- the card, the ledger, the shelf index, fleet-loop.sh -- had moved far enough since that re-applying them would have overwritten peers. Those four I reconciled by hand.
think THE PARKED COMMIT HAD TO COME TOO, BECAUSE A SHELF DESCRIBED A REPAIR THAT WAS NOT IN THE TREE. c842343cb held the TWO_ROOMS section the recovered REDS shelf claims as its repair. A cherry-pick refused over my edited shelf index, so I took its five content paths by tree read -- git checkout <ref> -- <path>, which carries the mode where git show would drop it (exec-bit) -- and wrote the two index rows by hand.
think THE LEDGER CAUGHT THE RECOVERY SQUATTING. reds_spine_derive read rebindings=1 squatters=1: my restored shelf claimed %538, and xy had spent %538 on a peer's row at 20260907.021143 while my lap sat in a stash. My row is unshared, so it renumbered -- and then AGAIN at the rebase, because upstream had spent the second number too while I worked. It sits at %551. Both moves were one sed over three files, because that lap had cited itself by stamp everywhere else; rule 4 of derived-spine paying for itself twice in one lap.
think THEN THE FIRE ROW'S OWN QUESTION, AND THE FIRST HONEST ANSWER WAS THAT MY CARRIED NUMBER WAS WRONG. The card said ASCII-first reads comments and never what a tool prints -- 5,511 lines across 1,584 files. That count reads every non-comment line, which sweeps in the grep patterns this tree's own guards must contain in order to find what they guard; 16 such patterns stand. A meter built on it would have asked the guards to go blind.
think THE POPULATION IS DECIDABLE, AND IT DECIDES ITSELF BY LEADING KEYWORD. Across 2,387 tracked .rish sources, 6,847 of 6,923 non-comment non-ASCII lines begin with exactly two words -- 5,260 say and 1,587 assert ... else -- and 76 are anything else. Those two shapes are Rishi's only ways of putting a sentence in front of a reader, so the meter counts them and refuses everything else by one gate.
think THE FAULT IS ONE EXCLUSION ANSWERING TWO QUESTIONS. Both comment meters refuse program content, and ascii_comment_witness gives the reason in its own GREEN line: converting a string changes what a program prints. A heredoc body is fed ONWARD and converting it changes behavior -- rightly excluded. A say line is said TO A PERSON and converting it changes register, which is the one thing the law governs. The braid single-stranded names, in the law's own instrument.
obs THE SIZE IS THE ARGUMENT AND IT IS A COMPARISON RATHER THAN A RATIO. 11,154 characters in 1,516 files, against the 505 the comment meter counts and holds in the same files under the same law. Twenty-two times is the ratio; the sharper reading is that 11,154 is very nearly the 10,468 that comment surface itself stood at on the day its own meter was built. Same tree, same law, one sweep less of attention.
obs TWO NUMBERS, BECAUSE THEY ASK TWO THINGS. 10,789 of 11,154 -- 96.7% -- are the six forms the rule's own substitution table names and spells: em dash 7,095, middle dot 2,788, ellipsis 665, right arrow 181, en dash 57, left-right arrow 5. The remaining 365 is typographic minus, section, double vertical, math relations, super and subscripts -- notation a reader chooses an ASCII word for. Reported as one total, the sweepable half and the judgment half would hide inside each other.
obs THE LOAD-BEARING LEG IS THE ONE THAT REMOVES THE GATE. Every refusal in the pen could also be explained by a plant the meter never met, so the control strips if (!spoken) next from a copy of the scan and reads the same pen again: that copy counts the grep pattern and the binding it must never see. Twenty-four keyed readings, nineteen affirmative and five refusals, on real git history.
obs I DID NOT SWEEP, AND THE REASON IS THE LAW'S OWN TEXT. ascii-first.md names three subjects -- document, code comment, commit message -- and a spoken line is none of the three. So this is a surface the law never named rather than a law being broken, and extending the subject list is Keaton's word rather than a meter's. The ceiling stands at the true reading of 11,154 and falls the day that word comes.
obs THE ONE RED I COULD NOT CLOSE IS SHAPED BY ITS OWN TIER. two_rooms_doorway reads 46 pages naming no room against a ceiling of 44, and it is tier cadence -- so no lap-tier cold pass has ever seen it, and it did not appear in this lap's 162. The recovered lap's SOURCE repair lands here; the 46 instances ask a room judgment the guard's own header declines to make, which is the question already standing on the card, now carrying a number.
obs THE REBASE COST MORE THAN THE CARD DID, AND EVERY COLLISION WAS A NUMBER. Upstream moved THREE times while I worked -- ff596816c, 9bceb12cd, 442dc597b -- and the same pins conflicted each time. A peer had already folded my own %540 under a different shelf name, so my fold was a duplicate and its shelf came out. The plant adoption floor had risen 5 -> 7 beneath me and my recovered control makes it 8, measured rather than guessed. shared_pen was lowered 60 -> 54 by one peer and its wipe ceiling raised 4 -> 6 by another; I took theirs whole and lowered files to 53 for the one file this lap repaired, touching the wipe number not at all.
obs FOUR RENUMBERS IN ONE LAP, AND NOT ONE OF THEM COST A SWEEP. The recovered row went %537 -> %538 -> %542 -> %549 -> %551, and this lap's own row %543 -> %550 -> %552; each move was one sed over three files. Eight ships share one counter, and a lap that runs three hours will be overtaken however carefully it fetches -- so fetch-before-book is necessary and never sufficient, and what actually pays is rule 4: cite the STAMP, and let the number be a view. Measured against the law's own figure of 20 to 36 files a shared %N reaches, the stamp saved this lap roughly a hundred and fifty file edits.
obs THE ROW COULD NOT LAND ON THE PIN AT ALL. construction/REDS.md stood at 40,333 of 40,960 with ALL TEN of its rows reading OPEN, so reds_fold.sh had no lawful move -- the pin deadlock reds_pin_capacity was built to report (%338). The row was born straight onto its own shelf, which is the escape that guard's own note describes and which upstream used four times today.
obs AND THE HAND-WRITTEN FOLD SUPPLIED THE FOURTH FIRING OF %474'S FAMILY. fold_shelf_link read fold_depth_lost=1 on my own shelf: the elder card row I folded carried [shelved](archive/20260907-013015_...), written at the card's depth, and from inside construction/archive/ that resolves to construction/archive/archive/. reds_fold.sh re-anchors links as its fourth step precisely because a hand forgets; a card fold has no such tool, so it forgot. One correction, and the card's own carried sentence -- every hand-written fold risks it, so tool the fold -- now has a fold of a DIFFERENT page behind it.
obs THE CARD COST MORE THAN THE METER DID. ITINERARY stood at 40,942 with 18 bytes of headroom at the open and at 40,956 with 4 after the rebase, so the row could not land until my elder account folded to a shelf. Nine trimming passes to fit 40,936; what fell was proof detail the REDS row and the scan header already hold, and what stayed was the finding and its two numbers. It closes 24 bytes under, which is a judgment call charged to whoever opens next.
obs THE ONE RED IN THE CLOSING PASS WAS THE PASS ITSELF. standing_equipment came back red at 72s with every other guard green; its evidence file names the cause -- assertion live_group_plant=ok, the pen could not make a holder with a live group leader. Run standalone against the identical tree it exits 0 GREEN. So the control plants a live process group, and it cannot get one while the roster it belongs to is saturating eight threads: a guard that reds under the very pass it exists to run inside. Pheromone's liveness-generation family (%471, %528), named here rather than claimed as closed, since the tree ships green and only the concurrent reading refuses.
